{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The bare minimum to get started with TanStack Form is to create a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TanStackFormController"},{"type":"text","value":" as seen below with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Employee"},{"type":"text","value":" interface for our test form:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface Employee {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  employed: boolean\n  jobTitle: string\n}\n\n#form = new TanStackFormController<Employee>(this, {\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: '',\n    lastName: '',\n    employed: false,\n    jobTitle: '',\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this example "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"this"},{"type":"text","value":" references the instance of your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LitElement"},{"type":"text","value":" in which you want to use TanStack Form."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To wire a form element in your template up with TanStack Form, use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field"},{"type":"text","value":" method of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TanStackFormController"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first parameter of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FieldOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" and the second is callback to render your element."}]},{"type":"code","value":"field(FieldOptions, callback)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Our completed test form should look something like below. The form collects first name from a user input field:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export class TestForm extends LitElement {\n  #form = new TanStackFormController<Employee>(this, {\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: '',\n      lastName: '',\n      employed: false,\n      jobTitle: '',\n    },\n  })\n  render() {\n    return html` <p>Please enter your first name</p>\n      ${this.#form.field(\n        {\n          name: `firstName`,\n          validators: {\n            onChange: ({ value }) =>\n              value.length < 3 ? 'Not long enough' : undefined,\n          },\n        },\n        (field: FieldApi<Employee, 'firstName'>) => {\n          return html` <div>\n            <label class=\"first-name-label\">First Name</label>\n            <input\n              id=\"firstName\"\n              type=\"text\"\n              placeholder=\"First Name\"\n              @blur=\"${() => field.handleBlur()}\"\n              .value=\"${field.state.value}\"\n              @input=\"${(event: InputEvent) => {\n                if (event.currentTarget) {\n                  const newValue = (event.currentTarget as HTMLInputElement)\n                    .value\n                  field.handleChange(newValue)\n                }\n              }}\"\n            />\n          </div>`\n        },\n      )}`\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Be aware that you need\nto handle updating the element and form yourself as seen in the example above."}]}],"headings":[]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThe bare minimum to get started with TanStack Form is to create a `TanStackFormController` as seen below with the `Employee` interface for our test form:\n\n```ts\ninterface Employee {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  employed: boolean\n  jobTitle: string\n}\n\n#form = new TanStackFormController<Employee>(this, {\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: '',\n    lastName: '',\n    employed: false,\n    jobTitle: '',\n  },\n})\n```\n\nIn this example `this` references the instance of your `LitElement` in which you want to use TanStack Form.\n\nTo wire a form element in your template up with TanStack Form, use the `field` method of `TanStackFormController`.\n\nThe first parameter of `field` is `FieldOptions` and the second is callback to render your element.\n\n```ts\nfield(FieldOptions, callback)\n```\n\nOur completed test form should look something like below. The form collects first name from a user input field:\n\n```ts\nexport class TestForm extends LitElement {\n  #form = new TanStackFormController<Employee>(this, {\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: '',\n      lastName: '',\n      employed: false,\n      jobTitle: '',\n    },\n  })\n  render() {\n    return html` <p>Please enter your first name</p>\n      ${this.#form.field(\n        {\n          name: `firstName`,\n          validators: {\n            onChange: ({ value }) =>\n              value.length < 3 ? 'Not long enough' : undefined,\n          },\n        },\n        (field: FieldApi<Employee, 'firstName'>) => {\n          return html` <div>\n            <label class=\"first-name-label\">First Name</label>\n            <input\n              id=\"firstName\"\n              type=\"text\"\n              placeholder=\"First Name\"\n              @blur=\"${() => field.handleBlur()}\"\n              .value=\"${field.state.value}\"\n              @input=\"${(event: InputEvent) => {\n                if (event.currentTarget) {\n                  const newValue = (event.currentTarget as HTMLInputElement)\n                    .value\n                  field.handleChange(newValue)\n                }\n              }}\"\n            />\n          </div>`\n        },\n      )}`\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBe aware that you need\nto handle updating the element and form yourself as seen in the example above.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: quick-start\ntitle: Quick Start\n---\n\nThe bare minimum to get started with TanStack Form is to create a `TanStackFormController` as seen below with the `Employee` interface for our test form:\n\n```ts\ninterface Employee {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  employed: boolean\n  jobTitle: string\n}\n\n#form = new TanStackFormController<Employee>(this, {\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: '',\n    lastName: '',\n    employed: false,\n    jobTitle: '',\n  },\n})\n```\n\nIn this example `this` references the instance of your `LitElement` in which you want to use TanStack Form.\n\nTo wire a form element in your template up with TanStack Form, use the `field` method of `TanStackFormController`.\n\nThe first parameter of `field` is `FieldOptions` and the second is callback to render your element.\n\n```ts\nfield(FieldOptions, callback)\n```\n\nOur completed test form should look something like below. The form collects first name from a user input field:\n\n```ts\nexport class TestForm extends LitElement {\n  #form = new TanStackFormController<Employee>(this, {\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: '',\n      lastName: '',\n      employed: false,\n      jobTitle: '',\n    },\n  })\n  render() {\n    return html` <p>Please enter your first name</p>\n      ${this.#form.field(\n        {\n          name: `firstName`,\n          validators: {\n            onChange: ({ value }) =>\n              value.length < 3 ? 'Not long enough' : undefined,\n          },\n        },\n        (field: FieldApi<Employee, 'firstName'>) => {\n          return html` <div>\n            <label class=\"first-name-label\">First Name</label>\n            <input\n              id=\"firstName\"\n              type=\"text\"\n              placeholder=\"First Name\"\n              @blur=\"${() => field.handleBlur()}\"\n              .value=\"${field.state.value}\"\n              @input=\"${(event: InputEvent) => {\n                if (event.currentTarget) {\n                  const newValue = (event.currentTarget as HTMLInputElement)\n                    .value\n                  field.handleChange(newValue)\n                }\n              }}\"\n            />\n          </div>`\n        },\n      )}`\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBe aware that you need\nto handle updating the element and form yourself as seen in the example above.\n"}
